	"FriendlyName": "ReadGuard - Is Your Text Actually Readable?",
	"Description": "The engine scales your text. It never measures it. There is no place in Unreal that says 'this line is nine pixels tall at 720p', and that is the line somebody on a handheld is trying to read. ReadGuard measures every text block your interface is drawing, right now, on screen, and answers four questions about each one. How tall is it really? Not the font size you typed, but what survives the font face, the DPI curve and every layout scale between the widget and the viewport - measured in device pixels through the Slate font measure service and then converted onto the smallest resolution you support, 1280x720 by default, using the project's own DPI curve. Under the floor - 14 px by default - it is a finding with the widget's name and the measured height, not a percentage. Does it fit its box? Desired size against allotted size, so a label that is clipped, that wraps where it must not, or that spills over its border is caught by measurement rather than by somebody noticing. This is the bug that arrives with translation: the box was built for English, German is longer, and nobody retests. Does it survive 200 percent text scale? ReadGuard actually sets the application scale, lets Slate lay out again, and takes the same two measurements a second time. What breaks only at 200 percent is reported separately, because that is the line an accessibility review reads. And what is the contrast against what is behind it? Relative luminance and the standard ratio, against 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large. The hard part is the background, and ReadGuard is deliberately honest about it: nobody knows what is behind a piece of text without the finished image, so ReadGuard reads the rendered frame under the text box instead of guessing at the parent widget's colour. It samples the captured frame, discards the pixels that are the glyphs themselves, and reports the mean and the spread. Where the background genuinely varies - text over a moving scene, over video, over particles - the line says 'background varies', carries the worst value it measured, and is an advisory, not an error. A contrast number that pretends the background is constant would be worse than no number at all. The same honesty applies to coverage: ReadGuard measures what is on screen. A menu nobody opened was not checked, so every report states how many text blocks were visited and names the screens it saw. Green must never be able to mean 'did not look'. The report draws on UCanvas from an AHUD, so it survives a cooked Shipping build and so that a tool for measuring interfaces does not add widgets to the interface it is measuring. The arithmetic - relative luminance, contrast ratio, effective pixel height, the large-text threshold, the verdict - is static functions over plain values with no world behind them, covered by automation tests and callable from your own tooling. On a build server it is one command: ReadGuard.Gate checks at 100 percent and at 200 percent, writes Saved/ReadGuard/report.json and ends the process with 0 clean, 1 warnings, 2 errors - the same three codes as LocaleGuard, AssetWarden, WidgetLedger, LoadLens, HeapCensus and BindGuard. ReadGuard measures against the usual accessibility thresholds. It does not make anyone compliant with anything and never claims to. Companion note: WidgetLedger counts what your interface costs; ReadGuard asks whether it can be read. CaptionCue produces subtitles and offers the options a console check asks for - text size, background box, line width, safe area; ReadGuard produces nothing and measures the whole surface, CaptionCue's output included. One gives you the dials, the other tells you whether the setting is enough. Win64, one runtime module, source included, no third-party code.",
